and priorContracts—which brings me to 10 total. There are 3 constant values (zeros appearing in different places), and I'm tracking all the invocations: basePremium, reduce, itemSurcharges, the arithmetic operators, comparison operators, Math.ceil, and the final calculation combining all the components.\n\nI realize the complexity is mounting with these calculations, so I'm going to step back and focus on what actually matters—extracting that surcharge reduction into a separate helper function would make the code more readable and easier to parallelize.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 18 tests green. Now let me refactor the `quote()` function.\n\n## Naming Evaluation (First Priority)\n\n- **Function name `quote`**: In an insurance domain, \"quote\" is the precise term for a premium estimate, and it matches the public API the spec/tests expect. **Keep it.**\n- **Internal observation**: `basePremium(items)` is a named helper, but the parallel concept — the sum of item surcharges — is an inline `reduce` inside `quote`. Extracting it to a named helper (`itemSurchargeTotal`) restores parallel structure and reveals intent (Rule 2). I'll also group the policy-wide modifiers so the final formula reads clearly.\n\n## APP Mass — Current `quote()`\n\nComponent breakdown of the body:\n- Bindings: `basePremiumTotal, surchargeTotal, sum, item, firstInsuranceSurcharge, loyaltyDiscount, followupDiscount` + params `customer, items, priorContracts` = 10 → **10**\n- Constants: three `0` literals = **3**\n- Invocations: `basePremium`, `itemSurcharges`, `+`(reduce), `*`×3, `>=`, `>`, `Math.ceil`, 5 arithmetic ops in ceil = 13 → **26**\n- Conditionals: 2 ternaries → **8**\n- Loops: 1 `reduce` → **5**\n\n**Total Mass ≈ 52**\n\nNow applying the refactor (Rule 2: reveal intent via a named parallel helper; Rule 3: consolidate the \"modifier = base × rate, conditionally\" concept):"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01BxfCrWY1qEN9a34atWyPio","name":"edi